  "text": "To find the sum of the \\( y \\)-coordinates of the intersection points between the parabola \\( x = 34y^2 \\) and the piecewise linear function \\( f(x) \\), we need to solve the equation \\( 34y^2 = f(x) \\) for \\( x \\) in the range \\([-1, 3)\\) and then find the corresponding \\( y \\)-coordinates.\n\nThe piecewise linear function is defined as:\n\\[ f(x) = \\begin{cases} x & \\text{if } x \\in [-1, 1) \\\\ 2 - x & \\text{if } x \\in [1, 3) \\end{cases} \\]\n\nWe need to consider the intersection points for \\( x \\in [-1, 1) \\) and \\( x \\in [1, 3) \\) separately.\n\n### Intersection for \\( x \\in [-1, 1) \\)\nFor \\( x \\in [-1, 1) \\), we have \\( f(x) = x \\). So we need to solve:\n\\[ 34y^2 = x \\]\nSince \\( x \\in [-1, 1) \\), we have \\( x \\in [-1, 1) \\), and thus \\( 34y^2 = x \\) implies \\( y^2 = \\frac{x}{34} \\). Therefore, \\( y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{34}} \\). The intersection points for this range are:\n\\[ \\left( -1, -\\sqrt{\\frac{-1}{34}}, 1, \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} \\right) \\]\n\n### Intersection for \\( x \\in [1, 3) \\)\nFor \\( x \\in [1, 3) \\), we have \\( f(x) = 2 - x \\). So we need to solve:\n\\[ 34y^2 = 2 - x \\]\nSince \\( x \\in [1, 3) \\), we have \\( 2 - x \\in (-1, 1) \\), and thus \\( 34y^2 = 2 - x \\) implies \\( y^2 = \\frac{2 - x}{34} \\). Therefore, \\( y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2 - x}{34}} \\). The intersection points for this range are:\n\\[ \\left( 1, -\\sqrt{\\frac{2 - 1}{34}}, 2, \\sqrt{\\frac{2 - 3}{34}} \\right) \\]\n\n### Sum of the \\( y \\)-coordinates\nThe \\( y \\)-coordinates of the intersection points are:\n\\[ -\\sqrt{\\frac{-1}{34}}, \\sqrt{\\frac{-1}{34}}, \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}, -\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}, -\\sqrt{\\frac{2 - 1}{34}}, \\sqrt{\\frac{2 - 3}{34}} \\]\nSimplifying the \\( y \\)-coordinates, we get:\n\\[ -\\sqrt{\\frac{-1}{34}}, \\sqrt{\\frac{-1}{34}}, \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}, -\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}, -\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}, \\sqrt{\\frac{-1}{34}} \\]\nThe sum of these \\( y \\)-coordinates is:\n\\[ -\\sqrt{\\frac{-1}{34}} + \\sqrt{\\frac{-1}{34}} + \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} - \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} - \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} + \\sqrt{\\frac{-1}{34}} = 0 \\]\n\nThus, the sum of the \\( y \\)-coordinates of the intersection points is \\( 0 \\). Since the problem asks for the sum in the form \\(\\frac{a + b\\sqrt{c}}{d}\\), we have \\( a = 0 \\), \\( b = 0 \\), \\( c = 1 \\), and \\( d = 1 \\). Therefore, \\( a + b + c + d = 0 + 0 + 1 + 1 = 2 \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
